WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.700
Welcome to 2 minute neuroscience, where I
simplistically explain neuroscience topics

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.200
in 2 minutes or less.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.980
In this installment I will discuss the pineal
gland.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.670
The pineal gland was given its name because
it has a pine-cone like shape.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:19.279
Unliked most brain structures, the pineal
gland is unpaired, meaning there is only one.

00:00:19.279 --> 00:00:21.839
It sits directly on the midline of the brain.

00:00:21.839 --> 00:00:26.369
The function most linked to the pineal gland
is the secretion of a hormone called melatonin,

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:33.410
which is best known for its role in regulating
circadian rhythms.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:38.149
The pineal gland is made up of secretory cells
called pinealocytes, which secrete melatonin

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:40.579
throughout the 24-hour cycle.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:42.621
Secretion is highest in the middle of the
night.

00:00:42.621 --> 00:00:49.620
It begins to decrease as it gets closer to
dawn and is lowest during daylight hours.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.399
This schedule of melatonin secretion is regulated
by signals from the retina about light in

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:57.379
the environment, which travel to a nucleus
in the hypothalamus called the suprachiasmatic

00:00:57.379 --> 00:01:01.960
nucleus and then via an indirect route to
the pineal gland.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:06.540
The main function of the suprachiasmatic nucleus
is to control circadian rhythms, and in addition

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.730
to sending information about ambient lighting
to the pineal gland, the suprachiasmatic nucleus

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:19.430
also uses levels of melatonin as a signal
to provide information about the time of day.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:23.040
Because melatonin levels are highest during
the hours of darkness, melatonin activity

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.000
can be used as a signal that circadian rhythms
should be in their nocturnal stage.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.340
If melatonin levels are high and someone is
still wide awake, it is an indication circadian

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:33.080
rhythms are not in sync.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.690
This might happen, for example, after flying
across several time zones.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:41.020
In this case, melatonin is used by the suprachiasmatic
nucleus as a signal to get circadian rhythms

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.780
back on track.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:46.720
Due to its close association with nighttime
and circadian rhythms, melatonin has also

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.060
been investigated as playing a role in promoting
sleep, but the true relationship between melatonin

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.290
and sleep is still unclear.

